00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:18.660
But we'd like to ask you about
your research in grad school

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.843
and your work in current role
and how that work evolved.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.550
- I'm happy to talk about that,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.460
but first, thank you very
much for having me here,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.400
I'm very excited to have this opportunity

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:31.670
to talk with you all

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:36.020
about a series of very
important topics to me.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.010
But to the research, so as a PhD student,

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.220
I worked in a field called opto-mechanics,

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:43.550
opto referring to light,

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:47.390
mechanics referring to some
sort of oscillatory motion

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.630
of an object in the field

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.540
where the light or in the
area where the light exists.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:53.930
So it turns out that

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.640
if you can confine some amount
of light to some geometry,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.540
and you can oscillate
some object within it,

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.310
you can produce an
interaction between the light

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.780
and the mechanical
object that's oscillating

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.220
in a way where they
sort of mutually affect

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.950
each other's behavior.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.890
It turns out that you can
use that system potentially

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:17.230
to do some very technologically
interesting things.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:18.200
For example,

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.960
do networking between
different kinds of devices,

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.690
perhaps quantum computing devices,

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.950
when they exist in the future,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.380
you need to sort of have
them talk to each other

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:30.980
and opto-mechanical platform

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.860
is an interesting one
to do that sort of work.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:38.770
It also turns out that if you
want to do something like,

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:40.930
test the limits of quantum mechanics,

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.260
if you wanna see sort of how
big a mechanical object can be,

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.560
a massive object can be and
still exhibit quantum behavior,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.510
sort of almost wacky physics behavior

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.010
that you don't see in your normal life

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:55.510
or normal wing scales,

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:59.150
an opto-mechanical system
is an interesting one to use

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.360
to try to really understand

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.090
what is the limits of the size of a system

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.040
in which you can see quantum mechanics.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.680
And so in my PhD,

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.840
I did things like,

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.570
have light interact with
density fluctuations

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:16.403
in a really special kind of way,

00:06:16.403 --> 00:06:19.677
that was sort of half of
my time in grad school.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.410
The other half of my time in grad school

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.250
was building a new,
very interesting system,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.370
which involved levitating

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:31.110
a drop of superfluid liquid helium,

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.960
this is a very interesting state of matter

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.850
that flows with zero viscosity.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:36.634
You can use very powerful magnetics,

00:06:36.634 --> 00:06:38.950
magnetic fields to levitate that liquid,

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:40.490
and you can shine lasers on it

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.860
where some of the light will
get trapped inside of the drop

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.520
and that light will interact
with the drop's vibrations.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.960
So now you have a new kind
of opto-mechanical system

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.300
that's well isolated from its environment,

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.830
and so well-positioned to eventually study

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.370
some interesting quantum
physics phenomenon.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.150
Now as a postdoc, I do
something quite different,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.330
but still quantum
mechanics, quantum physics

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:06.930
is at the heart of what I study.

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:10.330
So to sort of introduce this,

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.910
it's useful to think
about solid materials,

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.743
a solid say a crystal,

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:20.420
it is a periodic arrangement of ions

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.790
where electrons hop back and forth

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.240
between the different positions of ions.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.580
Of the hopping of those
electrons around the material,

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.210
determines a lot of the
materials properties.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.300
Now you can make a lattice
completely out of light,

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.487
this is what I do now,

00:07:36.487 --> 00:07:39.040
and you can make atoms
really, really cold,

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.000
you can throw those atoms into
that lattice made of light,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.970
and those atoms will
hop around the lattice.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.160
And it turns out that this
platform is a nice system

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.410
to sort of simulate a real solid crystal,

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:54.690
but our system is on a larger scale,

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.270
it's easier to image,

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:59.500
everything is slowed down
and more easily measurable

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.150
with our current technology,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:02.700
and so we can use these

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.300
sort of quantum simulation experiments

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.550
to gain insight into the
quantum physics of materials

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.570
in a way that is difficult

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:15.320
for people that study this sort of physics

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:16.770
in solid materials.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.760
And it's all sort of a platform
to start learning about how,

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.260
when you have a large system
with many interacting particles

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.370
where quantum mechanics is important,

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.290
sort of how does the system behave?

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:31.150
There's lots of open and very
interesting questions there,

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.620
and so now I'm geared

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.323
towards trying to address
those sorts of questions.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.753
- Very fascinating, Charles, wow.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.150
Mathew what do you think?

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:46.983
- Yeah, no, thank you.

00:08:46.983 --> 00:08:49.867
Sorry (laughing drowns speaker's words)

00:08:49.867 --> 00:08:51.560
problems that we have.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:52.860
No, that was amazing Charles.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.460
Can you talk a little
bit about the sort of

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.900
move that you've made in your own career,

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:02.010
and I wanna talk a little bit about

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:05.453
the hashtag BlackInPhysics
week that you started.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.590
Some of our fellow Alumni
Association board members

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:11.860
actually came across your article,

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:12.730
which is part of the reason

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.042
why we wanted to bring you

00:09:14.042 --> 00:09:17.080
to be in conversation
with us this evening.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:22.080
And you've written in you're
somewhat scholarly work,

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.090
I would still call it that,

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:27.150
about your own experiences
with bias and anti-blackness

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.440
within the academy.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.800
And so as a result of that,

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.010
those experiences, you started
hashtag black in physics.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.890
Can you tell us a little bit
more about the initiative,

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.240
but also about some of those experiences

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.290
that you felt both as a graduate student

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.820
and in your current role as a postdoc,

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:44.803
but more generally as a physicist?

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.710
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:51.053
So I actually have a lot to
say here, as you might imagine,

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:56.980
but I think I can sort of
quickly sum up my experiences

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.543
in the academy with the idea that,

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:05.037
as I wrote in the "Physics
Today" article you're referencing

00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:08.340
"This and Taking the
Anti-blackness from Physics,"

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.662
many of my experiences as a student,

00:10:10.662 --> 00:10:13.473
involved people who had
a hard time understanding

00:10:13.473 --> 00:10:16.280
that I was part of the
intellectual community.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.950
And so it was often the case

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:22.360
that I was sort of thought to
not belong in certain spaces

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:24.210
and that made for very
difficult interactions,

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.790
and it was sort of the
buildup of this over years,

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:29.620
that was very difficult.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.500
And you know, this is not a
problem you'd meet to Yale,

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.020
this is a problem that sort of happens

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:38.560
when you have a very
small number of people

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:39.900
that look like me

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:43.960
in a space where the people

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.110
who make up the majority
of the institution,

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.730
aren't used to seeing
people like me around.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.910
Or maybe haven't even had
many previous experiences

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:55.190
interacting with say black people,

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.030
oh, that was surprising,

00:10:57.030 --> 00:11:00.450
let alone in an intellectual capacity.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:01.892
So I think that has summed
up many of my experiences

00:11:01.892 --> 00:11:04.080
as a student at Yale,

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.640
and it has also played a part
in some of my experiences

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.503
as a postdoc here at Berkeley.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:15.753
So let me address BlackInPhysics.

00:11:17.122 --> 00:11:22.122
So I guess the question is
sort of why did it come about?

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:27.310
Well, BlackInPhysics was
born during a moment of chaos

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.510
as a way for the community
of black physicists

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.080
to come together and gain a foothold

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:36.000
as the world around us
seem to be on shaky ground.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.280
Another organization,

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.510
the National Society of Black Physicists

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:41.290
started in an important aspect

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.220
of this community building work,

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.770
and it's been doing so for decades,

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.260
but on our side,

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.780
we wanted to sort of further
build such a community

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.710
based on modern social media tools

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:55.050
that grant a new level of global access,

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:59.240
and allow for new ways
for distant individuals

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.973
to engage and remain
engaged with each other.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.310
Part of our goal with BlackInPhysics

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.670
is to paint a more complete picture

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:10.040
of what a physicist looks like.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.870
Unfortunately only about
3% of physics bachelors

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.110
go to black physicists,

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:17.510
and only 2% of PhDs go
to black physicists.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:20.360
This necessarily means that
the number of black physicists

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.120
in lab directorship roles,

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:25.370
either in academia or industry is small.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.590
And it was just last year

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:31.210
in the entire multi-sensory
history of US higher education

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:32.370
that we crossed the threshold

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:35.680
of a hundred US born
African-American women,

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.143
having attained PhDs in
physics or astronomy.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.970
And so, even though our numbers are small,

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:44.920
black physicists have
played an important role

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:46.830
in the history of physics,

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:48.380
whether it be their roles

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.310
as sort of research associates in labs,

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:54.330
say students or postdocs
or their equivalent,

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:57.970
or as principal investigators
directing research labs.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:12:58.850
And it turns out

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:00.857
that Yale has an important
role in the system.

00:13:00.857 --> 00:13:04.350
The first Black American
to receive a PhD in the US

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:05.867
was a man by the name of Edward Bouchet,

00:13:05.867 --> 00:13:10.867
and it was awarded in physics
by Yale in I believe 1876,

00:13:12.571 --> 00:13:14.687
with its history as a
leader in many fields,

00:13:14.687 --> 00:13:18.630
it's a great influence, not
only in the academic sphere,

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:22.260
but also in the larger societal sphere.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.760
Yale is given its great resources

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.250
well-positioned to truly
lead and attracting

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:32.190
and importantly retaining diverse talent

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:33.890
that will level the playing field.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:37.640
So yeah, with BlackInPhysics,

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:38.820
we're not only shining a light

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:41.350
on the important contributions
of these physicists

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:45.740
by for example, leading
Wikipedia editing campaigns

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:48.460
that produce pages for
accomplished scientists

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.830
who previously either had no page

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:53.370
or whose page was only sparsely populated,

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:55.300
but we also provide a microphone

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:57.810
to amplify the voices of these physicists,

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:02.270
who are widely read, highly
visible series of publications

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:03.780
that appear both in physics today

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.403
in physics world magazines,
which have global reach.

00:14:07.404 --> 00:14:09.580
At this point we've reached a point

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:10.770
where we've made it difficult

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.933
to not see all of the many
talented black physicists

00:14:13.933 --> 00:14:15.560
who are highly qualified

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:17.860
to hold principal investigating roles,

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.010
such as professorships

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:22.050
or staff scientist
positions at national labs,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.820
or research team leads in industry.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.750
And we've aided at making it quite clear

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:29.750
that the talent exists
and where to find it.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:30.583
The next step

00:14:30.583 --> 00:14:33.800
is to truly value black
physicist contributions,

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:35.140
and to understand that

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:40.000
in order to continue
to grow and be better,

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.560
physics as a discipline must make space,

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:46.780
healthy and welcoming space,

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:50.560
supportive and respectful
space for black physicists,

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:53.650
so that only can the
discipline reap the benefits

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:55.330
of our much needed perspective,

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:56.780
but also so that black physicists

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:59.236
can reap the socioeconomic benefit

00:14:59.236 --> 00:15:02.570
of having highly valuable
technical knowledge

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:05.430
in a highly tech driven society

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:07.770
that constantly faces moral dilemmas

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:10.130
that affect many different
groups of people,

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:12.050
but typically only have
one group of people

00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:13.840
making the decisions,

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.140
often with an unacceptably,

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.250
low understanding of how
people from different groups

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:19.950
are affected with those decisions.

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:23.210
I know that was sort of a long spiel,

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:24.767
but that's my take on BlackInPhysics,

00:15:24.767 --> 00:15:27.830
and sort of that is why we
are doing what we are doing

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:29.330
and why our work is important.

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.200
- I really appreciate that.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.340
And can you speak a little bit

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:38.550
to the way in which there's pressure

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:42.050
both from within and beyond
the academy around this?

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:43.570
I'm not a hundred percent sure

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:45.480
about your own experiences with this,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:47.670
but you know, I know for graduate students

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:48.503
who are currently at Yale,

00:15:48.503 --> 00:15:49.336
there's a lot of thought

00:15:49.336 --> 00:15:51.580
into whether or not you
stay within the academy

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:54.420
and you become a faculty
member or to leave the academy.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.720
And has that played a role at all

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:01.160
or has BlackInPhysics helped you navigate

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:04.950
the sort of career trajectory
that you've decided to pursue?

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:06.880
- Yeah, so I think BlackInPhysics

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:11.035
has helped me navigate
my career trajectory,

00:16:11.035 --> 00:16:11.928
before BlackInPhysics,

00:16:11.928 --> 00:16:14.960
it was the National Society
of Black Physicists.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.953
And when I first started graduate school,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:20.630
I had decided to go to graduate school

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.500
because I said to myself,

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:25.200
I want to work as a
professional physicist.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:28.530
And typically in order to do
professional physicists work,

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:29.550
you would have a PhD,

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.700
and so I found myself in grad school.

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:32.830
Throughout my grad school process,

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:34.710
especially given the difficulties,

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:37.563
especially given, my difficulty with,

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:41.290
having a lot of messaging

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:44.010
that suggested that I
didn't belong in physics,

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:47.010
I sort of went through this
very windy rollercoaster

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:50.200
of, I need to get out of here ASAP,

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.860
and you know, I need to
be a part of academia,

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:55.423
and not only do excellent physics,

00:16:55.423 --> 00:16:58.330
but also like really take part

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:00.940
in shepherding the future of the academy,

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:03.990
it's a very windy path between those two.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:04.950
And when I first went

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:08.430
to the National Society of
Black Physicists conference,

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:11.483
which Meg Urry, actually
sort of made it happen.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:16.070
That was my first time seeing
so many black scientists,

00:17:16.070 --> 00:17:17.320
so many black physicists,

00:17:18.303 --> 00:17:19.950
that was my first time meeting any,

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:23.830
and that was a completely
like life-changing experience.

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:27.490
And so that was something
that really like,

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:29.360
sort of put it in my mind and heart,

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:32.240
that this physics thing is
something that I can really do,

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:34.910
and I think that my
work with BlackInPhysics

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:39.260
has sort of further strengthen
that feeling in my mind

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.813
So now I'm sort of taking a
more of a leadership role.

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:45.500
I've sort of certainly benefit

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.540
from the resources that we provide,

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:48.923
but I'm gathering people,

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.870
I'm sort of deepening
my anchor to the field

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:56.420
and I'm helping pull up younger people

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:59.054
and trying to make sure at least

00:17:59.054 --> 00:18:01.250
that some of the problems
that I dealt with

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:05.730
are less effective at pushing people out,

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.483
or maybe even upset.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.093
- Very interesting, wow.

00:18:14.096 --> 00:18:17.400
So I was reading your
physics today commentary

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:21.780
about disentangling, the
anti-blackness from physics,

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:23.390
and you cited how,

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:26.160
you were one of roughly
twenty-five black men

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:29.060
in Yale Grad School at the time 2015,

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.450
but you also just now

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:34.860
you talked about meeting
other like-minded people

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:39.860
and energized you and gave
you confidence and conviction.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:44.620
So I'm curious how you
kind of transitioned

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:47.490
and got inspired to actually reaching out

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.233
and to build a community,

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:55.920
and what kind of advice you
would have for current students

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:59.810
who may have similar
experiences in grad school,

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:04.330
they may be in physics
or other science area,

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:07.313
and how like what ways then,

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:11.520
how can they also lend to build community

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:13.193
and leverage each other?

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:16.850
- So yeah, that's an excellent question,

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:18.763
incredibly important for students.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:21.980
Really for everyone,

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.770
and it happens to turn
out that some of us,

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:27.680
in particular, ethnic
minorities or gender minorities

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:31.000
or other underrepresented folks on campus,

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.790
don't always find
themselves in communities

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:36.260
in the way that more represented people do

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:37.600
sort of automatically.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:42.600
And so I made a point to build
community out of necessity,

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:44.650
because I didn't have it,

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:47.860
and I didn't initially
realize the importance of it,

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:50.580
and it really became clear that

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:53.170
I needed to be able to
talk to other people

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:54.683
about my experiences,

00:19:56.410 --> 00:20:00.730
people that sort of automatically
understood my experiences,

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:02.240
definitely people who I could talk to,

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:06.250
that like didn't hold
preconceived notions,

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:08.530
say of my like intellectual abilities,

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:11.200
really could take me at face value

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.670
and respect me fully as a human

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:15.580
with an educated one

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:20.500
that has lots of useful
and important thoughts

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:21.870
to contribute.

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:25.780
And so my first sort of
major go with this at Yale

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:29.260
was the formation of the Yale
League of Black Scientists.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:30.410
So this was a student group

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:34.113
that I formed with two
other Yale graduates.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.430
One is my wife, Lindsay McMillan Brown,

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:38.500
who I believe is in the audience.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.460
She got her PhD in chemical engineering

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.600
a little bit before me, I
can't remember when anymore,

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.380
but then also Brooke Russell

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:49.700
who got her PhD from Yale Physics,

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:51.390
and she was the first
black woman to get her PhD

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:54.070
from Yale Physics, she was in my cohort.

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:57.970
So we started this group and it was great

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.560
because it was a mechanism

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:02.990
through which we can apply
for funds in the university,

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:05.553
through the very nice
student group system at Yale,

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:08.630
in order to like create programming

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:11.673
that can support black STEM PhD students,

00:21:13.043 --> 00:21:14.920
and that could be like sort
of professional development,

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:17.720
which we did, or also even social events,

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:20.963
because a lot of the
black students at Yale

00:21:20.963 --> 00:21:22.920
were in humanities and social sciences,

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:24.810
and they're on a different part of campus.

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:27.090
So as a grad student
it's easy to be isolated,

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:29.470
if you're a black STEM PhD student,

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:32.200
I mean the combination of
being physically far away

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:33.650
from other black students,

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:35.830
and there being few
black STEM PhD students,

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:38.540
I mean, it's very, very
easy for you to be alone.

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.400
And so Yale League of Black Scientists

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:43.460
was a mechanism to produce programming

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:46.320
that sort of supported our
professional development

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.320
as PhD students, as black ones

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:52.012
that helped us socialize with each other,

00:21:52.012 --> 00:21:54.490
and that kept us engaged
in the local community.

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:55.620
So another thing we did,

00:21:55.620 --> 00:22:00.620
was we got very engaged with
the pathways to science office,

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:04.370
just sort of attached to the
main outreach arm of Yale,

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:05.980
and we were everywhere around the city,

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:07.600
we were in local schools

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:11.350
and we were interacting with
kids from local schools here,

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:14.090
not here not at Yale,
but on the Yale Campus.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:16.100
And that was actually
a really important part

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:18.950
of providing anchors for us,

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:20.810
that sort of work made it clear

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:22.657
that our presence there was important,

00:22:22.657 --> 00:22:25.400
and that we were making important changes.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:28.280
And so this was sort of long winded,

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.197
but I can say that
community is super important

00:22:31.197 --> 00:22:33.780
and everyone needs to win.

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:35.950
Some people sort of get it for free,

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:38.490
and it tends to be the case
that underrepresented people

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.910
have to work for it or even build it,

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:44.320
because in many cases it
doesn't already exist.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:48.700
And so one good way for
students to find community

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:52.450
is to, well go run and look
for the resources that exists.

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:55.400
Yale has many excellent resources,
especially for students.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.850
And if you find something
that you need doesn't exist,

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:01.880
build it, and I found that
there were many people,

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.665
both in the physics
department or higher up,

00:23:03.665 --> 00:23:05.140
including Dean Cooley,

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.170
who supported the Yale
League of Black Scientists,

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.700
and try to make sure that we
had the resources we needed

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:10.750
to support ourselves.

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:14.740
This also means going
and talking with people,

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:16.100
leaning on people,

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:19.480
being authentic and real
about your experiences.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:23.260
I mean, Yale students are strong students,

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:24.677
who have histories of
being strong students,

00:23:24.677 --> 00:23:27.443
and sometimes go to yell
and have a hard time,

00:23:27.443 --> 00:23:30.220
and can have a hard time,

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:32.390
being honest about having a hard time.

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:33.480
But I think in order to make

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:35.700
the sort of connections you
need and build a community,

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:38.500
you need to really thrive as a human,

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:41.073
you need to be open with
other people, reach out,

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:42.783
and let it be known

00:23:42.783 --> 00:23:45.040
that it's okay to sort
of struggle with things.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:49.400
And so that may involve
going and having real,

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:51.690
maybe even heartfelt
conversations with your peers

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:53.610
or reaching out to faculty.

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:54.760
That's something that I did a lot,

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:57.680
I was fortunate to have
a fantastic PhD advisor

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:00.867
who I spoke to about everything,
I was lucky to have that.

00:24:00.867 --> 00:24:04.560
But there are other people
on the Yale Physics Faculty

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.263
that were incredible mentors to me,

00:24:06.263 --> 00:24:08.440
that I reached out to about my issues,

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:10.350
and they supported me in
the way that they could.

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:11.947
And so that was another
way of building community,

00:24:11.947 --> 00:24:14.200
and so my message to students would be,

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:16.470
identify the faculty that care about you,

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:17.764
there will be several

00:24:17.764 --> 00:24:22.150
and there are well-resourced
influential and powerful people

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:24.607
that can make very serious
changes in your life

00:24:24.607 --> 00:24:25.440
for the better.

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.460
- Wow, thank you for sharing,

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:32.023
I like what you said, being authentic,

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:37.067
be authentic about our hardship sometimes,

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:44.140
be willing to open up
and also to seek out.

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:49.100
Yeah, I think like I was
also in a way a minority

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:53.743
when I was there and, I
mean, I didn't know better,

00:24:55.926 --> 00:24:58.420
I was speaking out and seeking out help.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:02.260
So it it's great that you
had a great support system,

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:03.860
I mean, in the way you build it yourself,

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.110
you'll reach out to your advisor,

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:09.026
to other faculties and to fellow students

00:25:09.026 --> 00:25:11.723
even in the humanities,

00:25:14.118 --> 00:25:15.480
that's very impressive.
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:19.500
And actually, if I could add,
I'll say that our friends,

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:21.260
so, I'm speaking as a physicist,

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:22.093
I would say our friends

00:25:22.093 --> 00:25:24.410
on the humanities and social sciences side

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:26.210
have been incredibly important to me

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:29.830
in a way that I couldn't
have known would be true

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:30.990
before I got to Yale,

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:34.090
and I had the privilege of
interacting with so many of them.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:35.320
They gave me so many tools

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:38.670
to sort of contextualize
my experience as a human,

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:41.610
they gave me language to
talk about important issues,

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:44.920
and so, I would have been worse off

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:47.190
if I did not have the
fortune of interacting

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:48.290
with so many people

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:50.260
on the humanities and social science side.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:52.293
Just wanna add that very
important, very important.

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:55.483
- [Jia] Thank you.

00:25:55.483 --> 00:25:56.780
- Really appreciate your candidness

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:57.900
about all of this Charles,

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:01.090
and that vulnerability that
I think a lot of students

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:04.220
will empathize with and sympathize with.

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:07.810
And also, I just really commend
you on the ways in which

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:09.300
you were able during your time at Yale,

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:10.960
to reach out to so many communities,

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:13.467
you were talking a bit about community,

00:26:13.467 --> 00:26:15.750
but the ways in which you created your,

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.020
well, you reached out to
the scholarly communities,

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:20.160
the National Society of
Black Physicists and others,

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:22.640
but also your department,
beyond your department,

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:23.700
the Graduate School Community,

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:24.533
but even the New Haven Community,

00:26:24.533 --> 00:26:27.793
talking about the pathways
program, which is so wonderful,

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:30.540
and I think you're a model

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.130
of what we hope for
graduate students will do,

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:35.360
and to re emphasize that the Dean's office

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:37.620
is here to support students in that way.

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:39.010
It's a wonderful to hear stories

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:41.230
of students really taking advantage

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:43.280
of all there is to get out of this place.

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:47.740
And you've referred a couple
of times to mentorship,

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:49.180
and you even mentioned Meg Urry,

00:26:49.180 --> 00:26:51.680
who I believe is on the call tonight.

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:54.310
And I wanted to ask you a
little bit specifically,

00:26:54.310 --> 00:26:55.260
because on the one hand,

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:57.400
there is the building of communities,

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:01.920
but also I wanna ask you
about how you found mentors,

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:03.830
and we talked a lot about sort of peers

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:07.680
and finding your peers, but
finding good faculty members,

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:11.760
both as a graduate student,
and now as a postdoc,

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:13.220
as you grow in your own career,

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:16.050
how have your thoughts
about mentorship evolved

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:17.900
and how you see yourself as a mentor?

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:19.623
And can you speak a little bit to that?

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:23.023
- Yeah, so let me first say that,

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:27.430
unequivocally mentorship
is incredibly important,

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:28.897
and it's important for everyone,

00:27:28.897 --> 00:27:32.423
and again, it turns out
to be the case that,

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:36.713
while mentorship is
important for everyone,

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.110
it's often that
underrepresented minorities

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:42.280
don't find themselves

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:44.504
in sort of important
mentorship relationship

00:27:44.504 --> 00:27:48.170
that they need to thrive

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:51.680
in a way that more represented groups do,

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:53.830
some of which sort of
get it for free even.

00:27:55.359 --> 00:28:00.359
So how did I end up having strong mentors?

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:04.380
It was born out of me being authentic,

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:05.740
and me asking for help.

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:08.730
Me describing the issues that I was having

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:13.730
and seeking people that
sort of could understand

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:17.760
what I was trying to describe,

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:21.033
didn't necessarily have
preconceived notions of,

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:24.770
I don't know, my background, my ability,

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:25.827
wouldn't necessarily be like,

00:28:25.827 --> 00:28:28.947
"oh, you're just being sensitive,"

00:28:28.947 --> 00:28:30.277
"that's not real,"

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:33.520
that really listened to me,

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:37.240
and I think I was fortunate
to get in a number of ways.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:40.070
I mentioned that I had
a great PhD advisor,

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:42.700
and that was really
important to my experience

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:45.570
and I think not all
students are as fortunate

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:47.027
to have such a great advisor.

00:28:47.027 --> 00:28:50.040
My PhD advisor was not
only a fantastic scientist,

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:51.060
but he is someone that like,

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:52.910
obviously very much cares about the people

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:54.470
that work in his group,

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:58.640
and he was very open to learning
a lot about my experience

00:28:58.640 --> 00:28:59.940
throughout my time.

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:03.160
And he made it clear that
I could be open with him

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.460
about the difficulties that I was having,

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:08.410
and I did this with other faculty.

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:12.010
I already mentioned Meg, I
was a teaching fellow for Meg,

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:14.220
for definitely one semester,

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:18.720
and we were having a conversation
about women and physics,

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:20.047
and I think I said something like,

00:29:20.047 --> 00:29:22.997
"okay, well, if you think
this about women in physics,

00:29:22.997 --> 00:29:24.967
"what do you think about
black people and physics?"

00:29:24.967 --> 00:29:27.280
And that turned into like
a five-hour conversation

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:28.113
in her office,

00:29:28.113 --> 00:29:29.490
where I pretty much just unloaded

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:32.660
all of my difficulties
on Meg and she listened,

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:34.370
and she gave me lots of resources.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:35.668
She made it happen

00:29:35.668 --> 00:29:37.320
that I went to the National Society

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:38.890
of Black Physicist Conference,

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:40.700
and had this life changing experience.

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:44.910
So for me gaining mentors has again,

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:47.730
been being authentic about the
difficulties that I'm having

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:49.150
and asking for help,

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:52.650
and not necessarily being
stuck on having mentors

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:54.748
that look exactly like me.

00:29:54.748 --> 00:29:56.740
I will be the first one to tell you that

00:29:56.740 --> 00:29:59.030
role models are incredibly important,

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:00.290
and so for that reason,

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:04.080
Yale should continue its
work even more aggressively

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.150
to say diversify the faculty.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:08.160
So I think it's important for students

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:11.420
like people who share my
identity, for example,

00:30:11.420 --> 00:30:13.390
to see a role model that looks like that,

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:16.020
however, in the current landscape,

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:18.420
students, and really
everybody at all levels,

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:21.000
they need to be able to
identify what they need

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:23.290
and find mentors that
speak to different aspects

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:24.610
of their personality,

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:26.680
or that can provide different resources.

00:30:26.680 --> 00:30:29.660
And so, what was the case for me

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:30.985
in my time at Yale and even now,

00:30:30.985 --> 00:30:34.900
is me finding multiple mentors

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:36.520
that speak to different
parts of my identity,

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:38.195
as a physicists and as a human,

00:30:38.195 --> 00:30:42.790
and together, I sort of have
all of my needs fulfilled

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:44.500
by this sort of network of mentorship,

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:45.333
and I think that

00:30:45.333 --> 00:30:47.930
that's a really important
lesson for students.

00:30:47.930 --> 00:30:49.810
Go and find people that care about you,

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:52.360
that can speak to different
parts of your identity,

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:55.003
and that will serve you incredibly well.

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.070
And again, I keep saying students,

00:30:58.070 --> 00:30:59.230
but it's not just students,

00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:01.370
like you still need
mentorship of your postdoc,

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:03.770
you still need mentorship if
you're a junior faculty member,

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:05.230
even if you're a full professor,

00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:08.080
I mean, you're going to
be engaging in things

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:10.390
where there is some
more experienced person

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:12.190
that's not necessarily older than you,

00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:14.960
but that can help guide
you through a process

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:17.850
where guiding is very necessary,

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.130
like this is sort of always the case.

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:23.107
So I think that message is
important really for everybody.

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:27.873
- Yeah.
- I often

00:31:27.873 --> 00:31:29.370
tell graduate students, in fact,

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:31.880
that Yale is just made up of people,

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:34.470
and that that's how you sort
of have to navigate this place.

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:38.710
You can't just talk about
Yale as an entity on its own,

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:40.400
but it's actually made up of individuals

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:42.400
and sounds like you've met many of them

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:43.740
who have helped you in your career,

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:47.000
and now you're helping as an
alum, so we appreciate that.

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:48.373
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:31:50.703 --> 00:31:52.100
So I think that was a multi-part question,

00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:54.230
did I answer all the
parts of that question?

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:55.485
- Yeah, I think we hit on some topics,

00:31:55.485 --> 00:31:57.210
and, well, there'll be more,

00:31:57.210 --> 00:31:58.670
there's some questions
coming through on the chat,

00:31:58.670 --> 00:31:59.890
I'll also put a plug in now,

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:03.410
so please continue submitting
questions to the Q and A,

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.993
but I'll pass it over
to Jia for the next one.

00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:08.060
- Thank you, Matthew.

00:32:09.028 --> 00:32:14.028
So, I was wondering, now
Charles, you're an alum now,

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:18.060
so maybe you are already
offering some help

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:22.543
and advice to students, fellow
students in physics today.

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:27.970
The other question is
you kind of touched upon

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:32.970
at the end of your last topic,

00:32:35.152 --> 00:32:38.003
I'm combining this with a
question from Stephanie,

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:41.290
I mean, it takes a lot of initiative

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:43.810
to build that network
of the infrastructure,

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:45.183
the support system.

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:49.290
Now, what kind of advice do you have

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:53.937
from sort of an institution level

00:32:53.937 --> 00:32:56.580
and an educational institution level,

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:59.330
what changes we need to put in place?

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:02.100
What kind of developments,

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:04.300
like what additional infrastructure

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:06.760
we should help to facilitate

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:11.450
to make this easier for
students to be inclusive,

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:14.083
for them, easy to find
the support they need.

00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:21.060
- Yeah, so I think that, so to me,

00:33:21.520 --> 00:33:23.800
and really important part of this is

00:33:25.980 --> 00:33:27.653
diversifying at different levels.

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:30.370
So one thing that I think is really great

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:32.530
that the Physics Department
has achieved recently,

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:35.670
is really increasing the amount
of women in the department.

00:33:35.670 --> 00:33:37.000
If I remember this,

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.380
I heard that the most

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:42.080
or the last incoming graduate school class

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:47.080
was maybe majority women,
maybe 55% women or something.

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:48.920
I think that's really
special for a department

00:33:48.920 --> 00:33:52.370
that for a long time,
hadn't had very many women,

00:33:52.370 --> 00:33:53.669
and that's important because,

00:33:53.669 --> 00:33:58.669
it's more so the case that
new women who are students

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:00.930
can go in and see themselves represented,

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.810
they have more people that
can speak to their identity

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:06.560
that sort of understand
some of the struggles

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:07.393
that they go through.

00:34:07.393 --> 00:34:10.550
There's already sort of a
foundation for community.

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:12.150
And that same kind of thing is important,

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:15.110
for example, for black
people, say me, for black men.

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:20.110
And so I think at multiple
levels, if we could have...

00:34:21.370 --> 00:34:22.655
So let me just use this as an example,

00:34:22.655 --> 00:34:24.843
because I think about physics,

00:34:25.870 --> 00:34:29.300
and let me use my identity as an example.

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:32.850
What I'm saying is we need to
have more black undergraduates

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:34.990
in the physics department.

00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:35.823
And so that means

00:34:35.823 --> 00:34:37.610
we need to improve our
recruitment efforts,

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:40.970
we need to have more
black graduate students

00:34:40.970 --> 00:34:42.230
in the physics department.

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:45.680
And so we need to improve
our recruitment efforts,

00:34:45.680 --> 00:34:49.080
we need to have black postdocs
in the physics department.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:51.497
And so we need to improve
our recruitment efforts,

00:34:51.497 --> 00:34:54.170
and the same is true for faculty.

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:59.170
And I think like if you can
pull several individuals

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:01.860
that represent these different identities

00:35:02.830 --> 00:35:04.220
into the department,

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:07.990
again, there's already a
foundation for our community,

00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:12.990
and it will start to change
sort of department norms.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:17.810
And then I felt like you're
getting into a situation

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:21.780
where it starts to become more practical

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:26.142
or at least easier to sort
of change the climate,

00:35:26.142 --> 00:35:28.070
to change the environment.

00:35:28.070 --> 00:35:30.950
When you're able to change norms

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:34.000
based off of having these
different groups needs met

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:37.173
in a way that's policy-based
and actually enforced,

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:42.380
I think you can start to
really create an environment

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:46.240
where people can sort of
leave baggage at the door.

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:48.277
So in physics, a lot of
physicists will tell you like,

00:35:48.277 --> 00:35:50.597
"oh, you know physics
should be apolitical,"

00:35:52.090 --> 00:35:52.923
blah, blah, blah,

00:35:52.923 --> 00:35:55.420
but like physics, science
is all human endeavors.

00:35:55.420 --> 00:35:58.190
There's so many aspects of our humanity,

00:35:58.190 --> 00:36:00.710
so many aspects of society
that creep in the door,

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:02.520
and that includes things like bias.

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:04.060
And so if you're changing norms

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:05.417
and policies in your department,

00:36:05.417 --> 00:36:08.540
that's sort of really can
leave bias at the door,

00:36:08.540 --> 00:36:10.273
then you can have something like,

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:13.260
more physics interactions,

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:17.200
in a way that really
allows people to thrive.

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:19.259
So I think I'm always going to say

00:36:19.259 --> 00:36:23.460
that we need to change norms and policies

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:28.220
along with bringing more
underrepresented groups in,

00:36:28.220 --> 00:36:32.210
so that they can have the
climate that they need

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:34.520
to be respected and supported,

00:36:34.520 --> 00:36:35.370
but then also make sure

00:36:35.370 --> 00:36:37.476
that these people have
resources that they need,

00:36:37.476 --> 00:36:38.350
which are gonna be different

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:40.150
for students or postdocs or faculty.

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:44.220
And I think that in my mind,

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:45.370
that's a very clear way

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:50.370
to really steer the
trajectory of the academy

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:52.710
as a whole in the right direction.

00:36:55.302 --> 00:36:58.100
- Thank you, Charles, thank you.

00:36:58.100 --> 00:37:01.380
A lot to think about how
to enhance the pipeline

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:06.380
and then really recruit diverse candidates

00:37:08.920 --> 00:37:13.920
through all phases of
institution, so thank you.

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:16.580
- Which also might mean
bringing them in, in clusters.

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:20.670
And again, there are lots of resources,

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:24.700
there are lots of examples
of where these people are.

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:25.533
There are other things

00:37:25.533 --> 00:37:26.980
that get in the way of bringing them in.

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:31.790
So we need to sort of attack
the problem more head on,

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:33.710
and bring in a lot of the talented people

00:37:33.710 --> 00:37:36.100
that are known to exist,
like they definitely exist.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:38.450
We need to bring them in
and change the climate

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:42.240
and make sure that they have the support

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:45.203
and respect and resources that they need.

00:37:49.611 --> 00:37:50.517
- Oops, sorry Jia.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:54.920
Your response made me think
of one of the questions

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:56.830
that was actually submitted in advance.

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:59.260
So I think I'll just ask that real quick,

00:37:59.260 --> 00:38:03.570
and we'll sort of pivot to the
Q and A from the attendees.

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:06.127
And so someone had submitted
a question that reads,

00:38:06.127 --> 00:38:08.527
"what two things would
you say to a URM student

00:38:08.527 --> 00:38:09.717
"who is considering,

00:38:09.717 --> 00:38:12.307
"but unsure about whether to
pursue a post-graduate degree

00:38:12.307 --> 00:38:16.860
"in science and or physics
and why should they do it?"

00:38:16.860 --> 00:38:19.283
I mean, I think it's a
really interesting question.

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:21.663
- Yeah.

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:30.890
So one thing that I sort of
time and time again come across

00:38:31.069 --> 00:38:34.400
in my conversations with
underrepresented students

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:38.940
is concern with paying
for STEM grad school.

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:42.510
And so what's really important
for these students to know,

00:38:42.510 --> 00:38:45.280
is that in almost all cases,

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:48.010
a STEM PhD will be funded,

00:38:48.010 --> 00:38:51.150
so that you will have a stipend

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:54.790
that will support you
paying for living expenses,

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:56.610
food, and rent.

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:58.630
And it sort of depends on where you live,

00:38:58.630 --> 00:39:01.810
whether or not that
compensation is enough,

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:03.990
there are many examples around the country

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:08.290
where students are working hard
to have higher compensation

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:11.410
to address issues with cost of living

00:39:11.410 --> 00:39:12.870
for example, in the bay area,

00:39:12.870 --> 00:39:15.720
the cost of living can be very, very high.

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:18.190
But underrepresented
students need to know that,

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:19.893
STEM PhDs are funded.

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:24.040
That's an important consideration for,

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:25.870
I think, many underrepresented students,

00:39:25.870 --> 00:39:27.370
it certainly was for me,

00:39:27.370 --> 00:39:30.640
as someone who, came
from humble beginnings

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:33.250
and certainly would be concerned about

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:36.550
say, taking out tens and
tens of thousands of dollars

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:39.050
in student loans in order
to pay for my education.

00:39:42.890 --> 00:39:45.340
I think another important
reason to consider

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:47.110
doing a stem PhD,

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:49.090
is that in our society,

00:39:49.090 --> 00:39:51.960
it being very technologically driven,

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:55.660
I would say it is clear
that more STEM education

00:39:55.660 --> 00:39:59.470
needs some more socioeconomic mobility,

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.720
needs some more opportunities.

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:03.830
And again, as someone who

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:06.670
has for a long time,
not only loved physics,

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:09.150
but has thought it to be important

00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:12.627
that I can have a career
where I could support myself,

00:40:12.627 --> 00:40:14.533
and in the future support a family,

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:18.800
I think, one should really consider

00:40:18.800 --> 00:40:21.500
that if you have passion to do STEM,

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:25.290
get the best and the highest
education that you can,

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:28.940
because that will really
not limit your potential

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:30.890
of what sort of work you can do,

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:32.020
your earning potential,

00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:33.670
and the sort of opportunities generally,

00:40:33.670 --> 00:40:35.460
you will have to move around

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:37.350
and have different sorts of jobs,

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:39.000
maybe doing hands-on science,

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:41.810
or maybe doing some leadership work,

00:40:41.810 --> 00:40:45.303
leading teams of scientists.

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:48.500
So I think those are the two things

00:40:48.500 --> 00:40:52.270
that sort of most quickly come to mind

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:55.630
that these STEM programs
tend to be funded,

00:40:55.630 --> 00:40:58.530
and that having a large
amount of STEM education

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:02.483
will give you lots of economic
mobility in our society.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:05.960
- I think those are
great pieces of advice,

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:08.600
and definitely the funding piece

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:12.970
is something we try to talk
about a lot during recruitment,

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.230
I think it's an unknown fact,

00:41:15.230 --> 00:41:17.853
but we try and tell it to the world.

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.170
And I wanna pick up on one
other thing that you said,

00:41:21.170 --> 00:41:22.360
you have this conviction

00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:23.870
and it's actually come up a few times

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:26.010
about how you've always sort of known

00:41:26.010 --> 00:41:27.926
that you wanted to be a physicist.

00:41:27.926 --> 00:41:30.423
And another question that we received,

00:41:31.907 --> 00:41:33.707
"given the scarcity of role models,

00:41:33.707 --> 00:41:35.667
"can you speak to the
moment you became aware

00:41:35.667 --> 00:41:37.467
"that you could become a physicist?"

00:41:39.220 --> 00:41:41.210
- Yeah, that is such an
interesting question.

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:44.450
So I feel like I can answer
that in a number of ways

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:46.940
that aren't exactly
consistent with each other,

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:51.823
so let me try to give a bit of
context to what I might say.

00:41:53.170 --> 00:41:56.930
In a sense I wanted to be a
physicist for my entire life

00:41:56.930 --> 00:41:59.730
long before I actually knew what that was.

00:41:59.730 --> 00:42:03.050
I was fortunate to have in my mother,

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:05.360
a role model who really loves science,

00:42:05.360 --> 00:42:06.500
who wasn't a scientist herself,

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:08.810
but loved science loved to
talk to me about science.

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:11.410
And so that got me interested
in science very early,

00:42:11.410 --> 00:42:14.650
and so I've been sort of
thinking about science

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:17.095
and dreaming of being a
scientist for a long time,

00:42:17.095 --> 00:42:20.040
even though I didn't have
any role models around me

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:20.900
that were scientists,

00:42:20.900 --> 00:42:23.100
nor that I interact with scientists,

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:25.310
and so I got to university.

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:33.680
So that is one answer,

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:37.510
the other answer is maybe
when I was in grad school

00:42:37.510 --> 00:42:39.390
and I went to the National Society

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:40.850
of Black Physicists Conference.

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:43.090
And for the first time was in a room full

00:42:43.090 --> 00:42:45.910
of many kinds of black
scientists, physicists

00:42:47.015 --> 00:42:49.810
for physics, adjacent people
in engineering or chemistry,

00:42:49.810 --> 00:42:52.340
or biological physics

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:57.340
who had very, very wide
backgrounds, very wide experiences,

00:42:58.290 --> 00:43:00.600
some similar to mine, some very different,

00:43:00.600 --> 00:43:04.450
but who all were in the same
room, talking about physics.

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:05.790
Working in different universities

00:43:05.790 --> 00:43:07.543
or government labs or industry.

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:11.800
And so that was a very sort of

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:17.890
like self-image reaffirming experience,

00:43:17.890 --> 00:43:19.920
like I've had this
feeling for a long time,

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:22.130
like, yeah, I wanna be a physicist.

00:43:22.130 --> 00:43:23.870
I used to love Albert Einstein,

00:43:23.870 --> 00:43:25.770
I used to love to read about his work

00:43:25.770 --> 00:43:26.820
and read about black holes

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:28.767
and it was a sort of abstract thing,

00:43:28.767 --> 00:43:30.360
and then I see all these physicists,

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:33.653
I'm like, wow, okay, this
is really a thing now.

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:42.033
So those, those are my
answers, those are my answers.

00:43:44.410 --> 00:43:45.710
- I think those are great.

00:43:46.570 --> 00:43:49.360
So this is a completely personal question,

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:53.920
but so how did that get into
your specific interests?

00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:55.960
And I'm not gonna try and restate

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:56.960
what you said at the beginning

00:43:56.960 --> 00:43:59.010
about your specific
work in graduate school,

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:03.460
but about like, which
sort of drew my attention,

00:44:03.460 --> 00:44:06.030
I was really intrigued by that topic,

00:44:06.030 --> 00:44:07.670
and the way that you were describing

00:44:07.670 --> 00:44:10.190
what it can do with certain materials,

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:12.900
but was there a sort of
pathway that led you,

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:15.920
and I suppose part of the way
in which I'm framing this,

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:19.140
I could ask the question of
like, why did you choose Yale?

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:20.770
And like what brought you here

00:44:20.770 --> 00:44:22.030
and how did you get interested

00:44:22.030 --> 00:44:24.730
in the specifics of the work
that you were doing here?

00:44:25.630 --> 00:44:28.790
- Yeah, so, you know, when
I first started college,

00:44:28.790 --> 00:44:30.580
I was actually an engineering major,

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:35.580
and I quickly realized that,
I needed to change my path,

00:44:35.950 --> 00:44:39.290
because it was the, how things worked,

00:44:39.290 --> 00:44:41.630
that was the thing that
sort of drew me the most

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:43.280
that I was most passionate about.

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:47.030
Now with that,

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:50.640
I sort of have always been
interested in the foundations.

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:53.240
I like to think of myself
as an investigator,

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:55.093
as a sort of detective in a sense.

00:44:55.960 --> 00:44:57.200
But instead of solving murders,

00:44:57.200 --> 00:44:59.110
I'm trying to solve
mysteries of the universe,

00:44:59.110 --> 00:45:01.370
and I happened to do
so through experiments.

00:45:01.370 --> 00:45:04.240
I used to want to do so theoretically,

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:06.240
and so that has led to my interest

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:09.090
in the foundations of how things work.

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:10.372
So when I got to the
University of Minnesota

00:45:10.372 --> 00:45:12.530
where I got my bachelor's degree,

00:45:12.530 --> 00:45:15.030
and I took my first quantum physics class,

00:45:15.030 --> 00:45:19.110
I had a great teacher, his
name is James Kakalios,

00:45:19.110 --> 00:45:21.190
he's a condensed matter experimentalists

00:45:21.190 --> 00:45:24.100
that works on different kinds of devices,

00:45:24.100 --> 00:45:28.110
but he also cares a lot about Sci Com.

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:29.420
He wrote a popular science book called

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:30.750
"The Physics of Superheroes."

00:45:30.750 --> 00:45:32.350
So that already sort of tells you

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:34.830
that he's developed his
ability to talk about science

00:45:34.830 --> 00:45:35.910
and engage in work.

00:45:35.910 --> 00:45:37.030
And he taught my quantum class,

00:45:37.030 --> 00:45:39.310
and after that I was completely hooked.

00:45:39.310 --> 00:45:42.145
I felt like I have to be
thinking about quantum mechanics

00:45:42.145 --> 00:45:44.020
and quantum physics
for the rest of my life

00:45:44.020 --> 00:45:45.580
after taking his class.

00:45:45.580 --> 00:45:47.520
And so I had a research
experience with him,

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:48.787
so I was fortunate to get into his lab,

00:45:48.787 --> 00:45:52.230
I had a summer research
experience funded by the NSF

00:45:52.230 --> 00:45:54.530
in their research experience
for undergraduate program,

00:45:54.530 --> 00:45:57.780
where I got to go to
U Chicago for a summer

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:00.690
and do some quantum related research.

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:03.590
I did a senior honors thesis

00:46:03.590 --> 00:46:05.690
where I got to do other
kinds of materials,

00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:06.523
quantum research.

00:46:06.523 --> 00:46:09.200
And so when it was time for
me to go to grad school,

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:11.190
I was completely sold that I was gonna do

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:13.023
some sort of material physics thing.

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:16.660
But in my process of searching

00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:19.200
for potential PhD advisor in grad school,

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:22.310
I came across Jack Harris,
who was my PhD advisor,

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:26.920
and I thought his work was incredible.

00:46:26.920 --> 00:46:28.100
That was incredibly interesting,

00:46:28.100 --> 00:46:30.390
I think when you come in
as a graduate student,

00:46:30.390 --> 00:46:32.820
you're really wide-eyed and you
think everything is amazing,

00:46:32.820 --> 00:46:34.240
and it's because everything is, right?

00:46:34.240 --> 00:46:36.570
Yale faculty and other faculty elsewhere

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:38.370
do lots of very interesting science.

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:40.540
But I was convinced

00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:43.845
that the person who became my PhD advisor

00:46:43.845 --> 00:46:45.950
was incredibly interesting.

00:46:45.950 --> 00:46:47.430
Now, a funny thing here,

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:50.660
is that I ended up working on
something completely different

00:46:50.660 --> 00:46:51.730
in his group.

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:53.420
I had this opportunity to switch away

00:46:53.420 --> 00:46:55.240
from doing material physics stuff,

00:46:55.240 --> 00:46:59.380
to thinking about this, light
interacting with matter,

00:46:59.380 --> 00:47:01.070
thinking about quantum mechanics,

00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:03.560
thinking about how big a
quantum system could be,

00:47:03.560 --> 00:47:06.620
which like really fulfilled my desire

00:47:06.620 --> 00:47:08.570
to think about foundations in physics,

00:47:08.570 --> 00:47:11.510
like deep, deep things about
why things are the way they are

00:47:11.510 --> 00:47:12.907
and what they can be.

00:47:12.907 --> 00:47:14.760
And so that's sort of what set me

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:16.943
on the quantum physics trajectory.

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:21.690
- I love that combination
of sort of breadth and depth

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:24.650
that as you sort of continued
going for the fundamentals,

00:47:24.650 --> 00:47:27.590
you were also sort of trying
out all of these different ways

00:47:27.590 --> 00:47:30.143
in which they can come
to bear, that's great.

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:34.400
So we've sort of touched on this broadly,

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:38.460
but there's a question that's
very specific to physics

00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:42.010
and it's, what are some of
the challenges in your opinion

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:44.590
that black underrepresented
students and scientists face

00:47:44.590 --> 00:47:46.293
in navigating the field?

00:47:47.170 --> 00:47:49.733
So thinking really
specifically about physics,

00:47:51.450 --> 00:47:52.620
are there particular things

00:47:52.620 --> 00:47:57.400
that you think black students
and scientists are facing?

00:47:57.400 --> 00:47:58.910
- Yeah, I think one of the biggest things

00:47:58.910 --> 00:48:00.273
is perceptions of ability.

00:48:01.200 --> 00:48:04.590
So unfortunately there,

00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:06.720
many aspects of society

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:09.350
tend to want to force
black people in particular

00:48:09.350 --> 00:48:11.330
into a certain box,

00:48:11.330 --> 00:48:15.480
and that box does not include
attributes like smart.

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:20.480
And that is a very real thing
that you feel immediately

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:23.280
when you walk into an
environment like Yale,

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:26.250
where intelligence is sort of a currency.

00:48:26.250 --> 00:48:27.890
Well, that's one of the main ways

00:48:27.890 --> 00:48:32.150
by which you evaluate
a peer or a near peer

00:48:32.150 --> 00:48:36.280
or someone you either
outrank or who outranks you.

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:40.610
And so certainly perceived intelligence

00:48:40.610 --> 00:48:44.710
or perceived incompetence is a difficulty.

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:49.530
It also turns out that race and
class, socioeconomic status,

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:54.350
are sort of all wound
up in the United States,

00:48:54.350 --> 00:48:57.080
and it also turns out that

00:48:57.080 --> 00:48:59.110
that can mean that for some students,

00:48:59.110 --> 00:49:02.550
some under represented
students, some blacks students,

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:05.850
they could come up in
an educational system

00:49:05.850 --> 00:49:07.713
that lacked important resources.

00:49:08.780 --> 00:49:13.770
And so I think what that means is,

00:49:13.770 --> 00:49:18.553
sometimes, I'm gonna really
careful about how I say this.

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:33.490
I think that means that sometimes

00:49:34.370 --> 00:49:39.370
students can find that they
have different preparation

00:49:39.900 --> 00:49:41.037
than other students,

00:49:41.037 --> 00:49:43.970
and sometimes they're not
in teaching environments

00:49:43.970 --> 00:49:48.970
where the teaching is sort
of cognizant of that fact.

00:49:51.620 --> 00:49:53.550
So sometimes you end up in a classroom

00:49:53.550 --> 00:49:55.450
where students have different preparation,

00:49:55.450 --> 00:49:56.550
and it's not always the case

00:49:56.550 --> 00:50:00.070
that the teaching is
geared towards sort of

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:02.520
meeting people where they
are and building them up.

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:05.180
Sometimes teaching can speak to

00:50:05.180 --> 00:50:08.810
only the students who have
had the most resources

00:50:08.810 --> 00:50:12.970
that come from the most
well-funded school districts,

00:50:12.970 --> 00:50:16.060
that have the wealthy people
who can pay the taxes,

00:50:16.060 --> 00:50:17.670
which then go and fund the schools,

00:50:17.670 --> 00:50:20.360
that give some students,
all these extra resources

00:50:20.360 --> 00:50:21.860
compared to other students.

00:50:21.860 --> 00:50:25.320
And that leads to
differences in preparation

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:27.820
when going into next steps.

00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:29.450
That's an issue, that's
a sort of bigger one,

00:50:29.450 --> 00:50:31.980
that's a, society-wide, that's a funding,

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:36.980
that's a, how do we,
yeah, that's a tough one,

00:50:37.870 --> 00:50:40.410
but that's important.

00:50:40.410 --> 00:50:44.190
And again, I've already
mentioned about community,

00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:46.750
again, it's easy to come into a situation

00:50:46.750 --> 00:50:48.780
where you were the only person.

00:50:48.780 --> 00:50:50.730
For over a decade,

00:50:50.730 --> 00:50:54.170
I have almost always been
the only person like me

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:55.030
in the room.

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:59.340
And it's really hard to
have that identity sometimes

00:50:59.340 --> 00:51:01.830
because I can tell you when
I was a younger grad student,

00:51:01.830 --> 00:51:05.650
I was definitely afraid
of making a mistake

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:07.660
and convincing everyone around me

00:51:07.660 --> 00:51:11.470
that what people thought
about black people was true.

00:51:11.470 --> 00:51:16.470
That if I was to fail a test, for example,

00:51:16.490 --> 00:51:18.510
it would only suggest that,

00:51:18.510 --> 00:51:20.463
it is indeed the case that black
people cannot be physicist,

00:51:20.463 --> 00:51:22.800
because you don't see
them anywhere, right?

00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:24.830
And people already sort
of hold these notions

00:51:24.830 --> 00:51:26.280
sometimes about us.

00:51:26.280 --> 00:51:30.670
And so black students or LGBTQ students

00:51:30.670 --> 00:51:34.410
or students with various disabilities,

00:51:34.410 --> 00:51:36.500
can sometimes carry this weight

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:38.120
where they don't see role models,

00:51:38.120 --> 00:51:41.030
people already don't think that
they belong in these spaces,

00:51:41.030 --> 00:51:43.230
and it's really hard for them to feel like

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:44.647
they can actually be
human and make mistakes

00:51:44.647 --> 00:51:48.860
without confirming that
they indeed do not belong.

00:51:48.860 --> 00:51:50.560
And so that is a huge challenge

00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:52.560
that you sort of always face

00:51:52.560 --> 00:51:54.460
when you're underrepresented students.

00:51:55.843 --> 00:51:57.090
I think I'll maybe I'll stop there,

00:51:57.090 --> 00:51:59.727
I can keep on going but I should stop.

00:51:59.727 --> 00:52:02.487
- No, I definitely, I appreciate that.

00:52:02.487 --> 00:52:04.120
And the ways in which

00:52:04.120 --> 00:52:07.990
we have this complicated
situation in graduate school,

00:52:07.990 --> 00:52:10.470
where there is this sort of,

00:52:10.470 --> 00:52:11.970
the way in which your
intelligence is perceived

00:52:11.970 --> 00:52:12.803
is not always accurate,

00:52:12.803 --> 00:52:14.880
and then there's also imposter
syndrome on top of that

00:52:14.880 --> 00:52:17.090
and how those two things
play with one another,

00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:18.510
and that pressure

00:52:18.510 --> 00:52:20.510
that so many underrepresented
students feel

00:52:20.510 --> 00:52:22.233
to actually be the best,

00:52:23.550 --> 00:52:27.240
and all of that combined creates
this stressful environment,

00:52:27.240 --> 00:52:31.533
so often, I really appreciate
you speaking about it.

00:52:33.481 --> 00:52:35.780
So I think we'll Jia,

00:52:35.780 --> 00:52:37.620
I see put a question in the chat

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:40.220
so maybe we'll use that
as our final question,

00:52:40.220 --> 00:52:42.257
which is, "what is your next move,

00:52:42.257 --> 00:52:44.977
"academia, industry, nonprofit,

00:52:44.977 --> 00:52:46.197
"are you thinking about this?"

00:52:46.197 --> 00:52:47.700
"How are you thinking about this?"

00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:49.050
I think this is a question

00:52:49.050 --> 00:52:52.013
that our students are
constantly wondering themselves.

00:52:53.030 --> 00:52:54.830
- Yeah, so I'm thinking about this a lot,

00:52:54.830 --> 00:52:57.660
and I've been thinking
about it for a long time.

00:52:57.660 --> 00:52:59.210
Academia all the way.

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:03.810
So I'm positive that I want to
be a professional physicist,

00:53:03.810 --> 00:53:05.163
I could do so in academia.

00:53:06.080 --> 00:53:10.010
I also think that the
university system globally

00:53:10.860 --> 00:53:12.610
is incredibly important.

00:53:12.610 --> 00:53:14.720
I think it's one of the
most important things

00:53:14.720 --> 00:53:16.410
that humanity has done,

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:19.160
and I really want to take a part

00:53:19.160 --> 00:53:23.510
in being a thought leader in
physics and quantum science,

00:53:23.510 --> 00:53:25.610
but also being part of the leadership

00:53:25.610 --> 00:53:27.310
of the university system,

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:29.750
again, shepherding it into the future,

00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:31.080
making sure that

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:36.500
the university continues
to be like a hotbed

00:53:36.500 --> 00:53:38.100
for the production of knowledge.

00:53:39.252 --> 00:53:41.410
This is important for
just how we understand

00:53:41.410 --> 00:53:43.500
our place in the universe as humans,

00:53:43.500 --> 00:53:46.590
but then also for how we
say produce technology

00:53:46.590 --> 00:53:48.640
that goes and enriches lives.

00:53:48.640 --> 00:53:50.013
And I think it's important for us to do so

00:53:50.013 --> 00:53:51.810
in a way that's equitable,

00:53:51.810 --> 00:53:56.810
that is not like increasing
or driving disparities

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:59.130
in different people's experiences,

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:02.000
doing so in a way that
levels the playing field,

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:03.160
that enriches people's lives.

00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:05.900
And I wanna take a part in
making sure the university

00:54:05.900 --> 00:54:07.967
that I'm at in the future
as a faculty member,

00:54:07.967 --> 00:54:09.980
and that does that,

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:13.780
and I wanna sort of take
the role as a leadership,

00:54:15.314 --> 00:54:17.155
the role of a leader

00:54:17.155 --> 00:54:19.500
and sort of be a role
model as a faculty member,

00:54:19.500 --> 00:54:21.500
maybe for other faculty members,

00:54:21.500 --> 00:54:25.120
regarding what being an effective
faculty member looks like.

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:25.953
I don't believe

00:54:25.953 --> 00:54:29.340
that that is just being excellent
at science as a physicist,

00:54:29.340 --> 00:54:32.270
I think it's being excellent at science,

00:54:32.270 --> 00:54:35.290
being a good teacher, being a good human

00:54:35.290 --> 00:54:39.230
that cares about making sure that

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:43.100
different people from
different backgrounds

00:54:44.370 --> 00:54:49.080
are in an inclusive space
that respects them as people,

00:54:49.080 --> 00:54:53.593
that really provides them
with the resources to thrive.

00:54:54.860 --> 00:54:57.360
So yeah, I'm gonna be a professor,

00:54:57.360 --> 00:54:58.847
I'm looking forward to it (laughs).

00:54:59.970 --> 00:55:01.920
- That's a fantastic
answer, thank you so much,

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:06.419
and we need you, so we appreciate
that path ahead of you.

00:55:06.419 --> 00:55:07.600
- [Charles] Thank you.

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:09.760
